    ],
    seasons: ['<b>zomer</b> (december–februari)', '<b>herfst</b> (maart–mei)', '<b>winter</b> (juni–augustus)', '<b>lente</b> (september–november)'],
    climateLeft2: 'Het <b>tropische noorden</b>, rondom Darwin en Cairns, vormt hierop een uitzondering. Daar liggen de temperaturen het <b>hele jaar</b> tussen de <b>25 en 32°C</b> en kent het klimaat <b>twee seizoenen</b>: een <b>nat seizoen</b> (november–april) met veel tropische buien en hoge luchtvochtigheid, en een <b>droog seizoen</b> (mei–oktober) met zonnig en droog weer. Kortom: Australië is een bestemming waar je het hele jaar door in de zon kunt leven.',
    climateRight: [
      'In de <b>zomer</b> wordt het in Australië warm, met temperaturen tussen de <b>25 en 40°C</b>. Ook in oktober en november voelt het vaak al zomers aan, net als in maart en april, wanneer de warmte nog lang blijft hangen.',
      'Rond juni begint het wat af te koelen. Hoe <b>zuidelijker</b> je reist, hoe <b>kouder</b> het wordt in de winter, al zijn dit geen extreme temperaturen zoals in Europa.',
      'In <b>Sydney</b> liggen de temperaturen in de winter (juni–augustus) meestal tussen de <b>8 en 15°C</b>. Elk jaar is anders: mijn eerste jaar hier bestond vooral uit zonnige dagen, terwijl het \'s ochtends en \'s avonds flink kon afkoelen. Afgelopen jaar was juist natter, met name in juni en augustus. In <b>Melbourne en Tasmanië</b> is het over het algemeen wat koeler en wisselvalliger. Daar liggen de temperaturen gemiddeld tussen de <b>6 en 13°C</b> en heb je vaker te maken met wind en regen.',
      'Wat veel mensen verrast, is dat <b>huizen in Australië</b> vaak <b>slecht geïsoleerd</b> zijn. Daardoor kan het binnen soms zelfs kouder aanvoelen dan buiten. Een warme trui en een goede jas zijn dus geen overbodige luxe in de wintermaanden.',
    ],
    sunTitle: 'Heads up! Zonsterkte...',
    sunText: 'De zon in Australië is extreem sterk. UV-waarden kunnen in de zomer oplopen tot <b>13+</b>. Goed insmeren met <b>SPF 50</b> is hier echt een must.',
    clothesTitle: 'Wat voor kleding neem je mee?',
    clothesIntro: 'In Australië kun je kleding prima vergelijken met Nederland: er zijn <b>talloze leuke merken</b> en vooral veel sport- en zwemkledingmerken. In de grote steden vind je <b>Westfield Malls</b> en grote ketens zoals H&M, Zara en Cotton On. Voor de zomer en lente neem je de essentiële items mee, en in de winter vul je dit aan met een paar dikke truien en een (bomber)jas.',
    lists: [
      {
        id: 'basis',
        title: 'Basis inpaklijst',
        sub: 'Essentiële items voor elke reis',
        items: ['Korte broeken / shorts', 'Jurkjes / rokjes', 'Luchtige topjes / t-shirts', 'Lange luchtige broek / linnen broek', 'Spijkerbroek', 'Hoodie / sweater', 'Blazer / dunne jas', 'Sneakers', 'Slippers / sandalen', 'Sportschoenen'],
      },
      {
        id: 'musthaves',
        title: 'Must haves',
        sub: 'Extra items voor een comfortabele reis',
        items: ['Zonnebrand SPF 50 (hier kopen, dus kleine fles mee)', 'Strandlaken', 'Hoed / pet', 'Zonnebril', 'Wereldstekker (type I)', 'Navulbare waterfles', 'Boek of e-reader', 'Powerbank'],
      },
    ],
    appsTitle: 'Handige apps voor in Australië',
    apps: [
      {
        title: 'Reizen & roadtrips',
        items: [
          ['WikiCamps', 'Dé app voor campings, free camps, douches en toiletten'],
          ['CampMate', 'Vergelijkbaar met WikiCamps: campings, parkeerplekken en faciliteiten'],
          ['Fuel Map Australia', 'De goedkoopste benzine vinden (heel handig in de Outback)'],
          ['Google Maps', 'Navigatie en offline kaarten downloaden'],
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'Openbaar & eigen vervoer',
        items: [
          ['Lime', 'Elektrische fiets of step voor korte afstanden in de stad'],
          ['DiDi', 'Goedkope taxi-app (download via de Australische App Store)'],
          ['Uber', 'Bekende taxi-app, overal in de steden beschikbaar'],
          ['Drive Mate', 'Huur een auto van particulieren voor een dag of weekendtrip'],
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'Bankzaken',
        items: [
          ['Valutaconverter', 'Eenvoudig valuta omrekenen'],
          ['Commonwealth Bank', 'Betaalapp voor je Australische bankrekening'],
          ['Wise / Revolut', 'Internationale betaalapps'],
        ],
      },
    ],
    back: 'Terug naar boven',
  },
  en: {
    eyebrow: 'Blog · Australia',
    title: 'Preparing for your <b>Working Holiday</b>',
    tocTitle: 'Jump to',
    toc: [
      { id: 'meenemen', label: 'What to bring?' },
      { id: 'zorgverzekering', label: 'Health insurance' },
      { id: 'betaalmiddelen', label: 'Payments' },
      { id: 'simkaart', label: 'SIM card' },
      { id: 'klimaat', label: 'Climate' },
      { id: 'kleding', label: 'Clothing' },
      { id: 'apps', label: 'Useful apps' },
    ],
    checkTitle: 'What do you bring to Australia?',
    checkIntro: 'Before you board your flight to Australia, good preparation is key. Make sure you have ticked off the boxes below, so you can start your trip stress-free.',
    checklist: [
      'Valid passport (valid for at least 6 months)',
      'Approved visa (e.g. Tourist, Working Holiday, Student)',
      'Enough funds to cover your first period (be able to show at least <b>$5,000 AUD</b> for food, drinks and accommodation)',
      'Copies of your most important documents in your email and cloud (passport, driving licence, visa approval)',
      `International driving permit (get it from the ${ext(URL_ANWB, 'ANWB', true)}, handy for road trips and vanlife)`,
      `Internationally accepted payment methods ${jump('betaalmiddelen', '(read more below)')}`,
      `Good travel and health insurance ${jump('zorgverzekering', '(read more below)')}`,
    ],
    insuranceTitle: 'Health insurance in Australia',
    insuranceSub: 'Working Holiday visa',
    insurance: [
      'Going to Australia on a Working Holiday visa? As a Dutch traveller you are entitled to <b>Medicare</b> through the <b>Reciprocal Health Care Agreement</b>. This means medically necessary care, such as a GP visit or treatment in a public hospital, is covered. Once you arrive in Australia you can apply for Medicare yourself on the official government website.',
      'Keep in mind that the cover is limited: <b>ambulance, dentist, physiotherapy, repatriation</b> and <b>work- or travel-related accidents</b> are not included. So it is smart to combine Medicare with <b>long-term travel insurance with work cover</b>, including the medical expenses option, so you are well protected for repatriation, ambulance and emergency care.',
      `There are special insurances for travelling and working, such as the ${ext(URL_ALLIANZ, 'Globetrotter insurance from Allianz Global Assistance')}. It offers this cover for trips of up to <b>24 months</b>, including working, medical costs and other extras.`,
    ],
    paymentTitle: 'Payments',
    payment: 'It is of course handy to sort out your credit and debit cards before you come to Australia. For travelling, living or working you can get a <b>Wise</b> and/or <b>Revolut</b> card. A local bank account with the <b>Commonwealth Bank of Australia</b> is a handy must if you are going to work here for a longer time, for example 6 months. That way you can easily pay and receive money in Australian dollars.',
    revolutTitle: 'Revolut & Wise',
    revolut: [
      'If you are going to travel, live and/or work in Australia, it is handy to create a Revolut and/or Wise account. You can add the cards to the wallet on your phone and pay contactless.',
      'The biggest advantage is that these apps let you easily convert euros to Australian dollars (AUD) with <b>low transaction fees</b> and a <b>good exchange rate</b>. Within seconds you can top up your account via iDEAL and convert it straight to AUD to pay in local currency.',
      'You can also easily send money to friends or colleagues, pay safely online and keep track of all your spending in the app.',
    ],
    cbaTitle: 'Commonwealth Bank',
    cba: [
      'Are you really planning to live and work in Australia for more than 3 months? Then I recommend opening a Commonwealth account. The <b>Commonwealth Bank of Australia</b> is one of the largest and best-known banks in Australia, comparable to ING or ABN AMRO in the Netherlands.',
      'A big advantage of an Australian bank account is that you can easily receive money from employers, make Australian bank transfers and depend less on international transactions.',
    ],
    cbaStepsTitle: 'How to open your account',
    cbaSteps: [
      `<b>Apply from the Netherlands.</b> I recommend doing this about a week before you leave, because done is done! On the official website you apply online for an ${ext(URL_CBA, 'Everyday Smart Access Account')}. You fill in your personal details, passport information and visa details.`,
      '<b>Choose a branch.</b> Pick a location close to your first accommodation. Staying in Sydney CBD? Choose a central branch such as Town Hall or George & Market Street.',
      '<b>Verify your identity.</b> After you arrive, visit the branch you chose. Bring your passport and the printed form you receive by email. A staff member checks your details and activates your account.',
      '<b>Done!</b> After verification you can use your account straight away. Your debit card is usually sent to your postal address within about 5 working days (this can also be a hostel or hotel address).',
    ],
    simTitle: 'Getting a SIM card',
    sim: [
      'You can easily buy a prepaid SIM card (physical or eSIM) when you arrive in Australia, for example at the airport kiosks of <b>Telstra</b>, <b>Optus</b> or <b>Vodafone</b>.',
      'Back then I got a deal from Optus at Sydney airport: for <b>$39 AUD (€23)</b> a month I got 40 GB of data, unlimited calls and texts within Australia and 400 international minutes. These days you can get Optus for just <b>$13 AUD</b> in the first month. I am happy with Optus and liked that they installed and set everything up for me on the spot.',
      `These days there is also a great deal at ${ext(URL_FELIX, 'Felix Mobile')}: <b>$20 AUD a month</b> for unlimited data, calls and texts within Australia for the first 6 months. At Felix Mobile you choose between an eSIM or a physical SIM. The physical SIM arrives within 5 working days. You can buy an eSIM in advance, but you can only activate and use it once you are in Australia.`,
    ],
    climateTitle: 'Climate',
    climateLeft: [
      'When I thought of Australia, I mostly thought of sunny, warm weather. Still, it is handy to know which season you arrive in before you leave. Australia has <b>opposite seasons</b> to Europe and the <b>climate varies greatly by region</b>. When summer starts in the Netherlands, winter starts in most of Australia.',
      'Most of the country has <b>four seasons</b>:',
    ],
    seasons: ['<b>summer</b> (December–February)', '<b>autumn</b> (March–May)', '<b>winter</b> (June–August)', '<b>spring</b> (September–November)'],
    climateLeft2: 'The <b>tropical north</b>, around Darwin and Cairns, is an exception. Temperatures there are between <b>25 and 32°C</b> <b>all year round</b> and the climate has <b>two seasons</b>: a <b>wet season</b> (November–April) with lots of tropical showers and high humidity, and a <b>dry season</b> (May–October) with sunny, dry weather. In short: Australia is a destination where you can live in the sun all year round.',
    climateRight: [
      'In <b>summer</b> it gets hot in Australia, with temperatures between <b>25 and 40°C</b>. October and November often already feel like summer, as do March and April, when the warmth lingers.',
      'Around June it starts to cool down. The further <b>south</b> you go, the <b>colder</b> the winter, although the temperatures are not as extreme as in Europe.',
      'In <b>Sydney</b> winter temperatures (June–August) are usually between <b>8 and 15°C</b>. Every year is different: my first year here was mostly sunny days, while mornings and evenings could get quite chilly. Last year was wetter, especially in June and August. <b>Melbourne and Tasmania</b> are generally cooler and more changeable, with average temperatures between <b>6 and 13°C</b> and more wind and rain.',
      'What surprises many people is that <b>houses in Australia</b> are often <b>poorly insulated</b>. So it can sometimes feel colder inside than outside. A warm jumper and a good jacket are no luxury in the winter months.',
    ],
    sunTitle: 'Heads up! The sun...',
    sunText: 'The sun in Australia is extremely strong. UV levels can reach <b>13+</b> in summer. Applying <b>SPF 50</b> properly is a real must here.',
